    "title": "Ex-Michigan GOP leader may challenge Rogers in Senate primary - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Brad LaPlante ,\n8 hours ago\nLANSING, Mich. (WLNS) — Former Michigan GOP co-chair Bernadette Smith has filed paperwork with the Federal Election Commission, setting up what is likely to be a 2026 Senate primary challenge to Trump-backed former Rep. Mike Rogers.\nSmith resigned from her position as co-chair last week. If she formally declares her candidacy, she would join a primary that includes Rogers, whom Trump, Senate Majority Leader John Thune, and the National Republican Senatorial Committee (NRSC) — the campaign arm of the Senate GOP — have endorsed.\nThe winner of the primary will face former Wayne County health official Abdul El-Sayed, state Sen. Mallory McMorrow (D-Royal Oak) or U.S. Rep. Haley Stevens (D-Birmingham), who are all vying for the Democratic nomination.\nThe seat will be left vacant by Sen. Gary Peters, a Democrat who is retiring at the end of his current term. It is one of three major seats in the Senate that Republicans hope to flip next year to expand their 53-47 majority — the others being an open seat in New Hampshire and Sen. Jon Ossoff of Georgia, whom Republicans see as vulnerable.\nRogers won the Republican nomination in 2024 but narrowly lost to Rep. Elissa Slotkin in last year’s election. Many have attributed Rogers’ loss to receiving 122,000 fewer votes than Donald Trump in the presidential election. Rogers, on the campaign trail, has been telling voters a single van in Detroit “with ballots” caused him to lose last year’s election.\nSmith did not respond to a request for comment.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WLNS 6 News.\n2",
